-- title:
--   Zero total drop forces monomial form in the crossing model
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a discrete valuation domain that is complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, let $\varpi \in W$ be irreducible, and let $e \ge 1$. Write $R = \mathtt{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,(\varpi^{e})$ for the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - \varpi^{e}$, and let `mk` denote the quotient map. Let $y \in R$ be nonzero, and let $ab = (ab.1, ab.2)$ be a pair of one-variable power series over $W$ with $ab.2$ having vanishing constant coefficient, such that the image under `mk` of $\mathtt{inU}\,ab.1 + \mathtt{inV}\,ab.2$ is $y$, where $\mathtt{inU}\,ab.1$ is the two-variable series supported on the monomials in $X_0$ with coefficients those of $ab.1$ and $\mathtt{inV}\,ab.2$ the series supported on monomials in $X_1$ with coefficients those of $ab.2$. Assume further that, for the order function $v = \mathtt{addVal}\,W$, the infimum of the set of dominant indices $\mathtt{dominantIndices}\,v\,e\,0\,ab$ equals the supremum of $\mathtt{dominantIndices}\,v\,e\,e\,ab$; here $\mathtt{dominantIndices}\,v\,E\,t\,ab$ is the set of integers $n$ at which $v(\mathtt{nfCoeff}\,ab\,n) + \mathtt{annulusWeight}\,E\,t\,(\mathtt{nfExponent}\,n)$ attains the infimum $\bigsqcap_{d} \bigl(v(\text{coeff}_d F) + \mathtt{annulusWeight}\,E\,t\,d\bigr)$ over all $d \in (\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to_0 \mathbb{N})$, taken for $F = \mathtt{inU}\,ab.1 + \mathtt{inV}\,ab.2$. Then there are a unit $w$ of $R$ and natural numbers $a, b, n$ with $a = 0$ or $b = 0$ such that $y = w \cdot U(\varpi^{e})^{a} \cdot V(\varpi^{e})^{b} \cdot \mathtt{const}\,(\varpi^{e})\,\varpi^{\,n}$, where $\mathtt{const}\,(\varpi^{e})\,\varpi$ is the image in $R$ of the constant series $\varpi$.
--
--   The statement is the rigidity half of the theory of Gauss orders on the crossing model $W[[U,V]]/(UV - \varpi^{e})$: an element whose Gauss polygon has total drop zero has no horizontal zero, hence is a monomial $U^aV^b\varpi^n$ times a unit. It is used in the single-slope analysis of Cayley–Hamilton cofactors, where it feeds the comparison of leading residues of the characteristic polynomial constant term with the dominant normal-form coefficient.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_exists_eq_unit_mul_U_pow_mul_V_pow_mul_const_pow_of_sInf_eq_sSup.lean

